L.push({id:"2.13",t:"Words, sentence, paragraph",
cards:[
 ["The method","This is a way to plan an answer by building it in three steps.","• <b>Two words:</b> one word for each point you will make.","• <b>One sentence:</b> a topic sentence that answers the question and names both points.","• <b>One paragraph:</b> the topic sentence, then each point with evidence and a sentence that links it back to the question."],
 ["A worked example","Question: How did Hitler dismiss the terms of the Treaty of Versailles after 1933?","<b>Two words:</b> Rearmament. Anschluss.","<b>Sentence:</b> After Hitler came to power in 1933 he rearmed Germany and united it with Austria, which defied the terms of the Treaty of Versailles.","<b>Paragraph:</b> the sentence above, followed by the evidence. The treaty limited the army to 100,000 men, yet by 1935 the army had grown to 550,000. The treaty forbade union with Austria, yet Austria became part of Germany on 12 March 1938."],
 ["Why it works","The two words stop you from repeating one point twice. The sentence makes sure you answer the question that was asked. The paragraph then only needs evidence and links, because the argument is already clear."]],
quiz:[
 ["What are the two words for?","One for each point you will make","The names of two sources","The first two words of your answer","Two dates","They become the two points in your topic sentence."],
 ["What must the sentence do?","Answer the question and name both points","Give a quote","List three dates","Describe the source","It is your topic sentence."],
 ["What does the paragraph add to the sentence?","Evidence for each point and links back to the question","A new argument","A different question","The opinion of the cartoonist","The argument is already set by the sentence."]],
practice:[
 {kind:"wsp",q:"How did the use of violence, intimidation and terror help Hitler gain power by January 1933?",
  model:"<p><b>Two words:</b> Strength. Fear.</p><p><b>Sentence:</b> Violence, intimidation and terror helped Hitler gain power because the SA made the Nazi Party look strong and because it frightened opponents into silence.</p><p><b>Paragraph:</b> Violence, intimidation and terror helped Hitler gain power because the SA made the Nazi Party look strong and because it frightened opponents into silence. Firstly, the SA had about 400,000 members by 1932, and its marches gave the impression of a disciplined movement able to restore order. This appealed to voters who feared civil war. Secondly, the SA broke up the meetings of other parties and fought Communists in the streets, which made it dangerous to campaign against the Nazis. Together these helped the party become the largest in the Reichstag in July 1932.</p>"},
 {kind:"wsp",q:"How did the Treaty of Versailles lead to World War Two?",
  model:"<p><b>Two words:</b> Resentment. Revision.</p><p><b>Sentence:</b> The Treaty of Versailles led to World War Two because it created lasting German resentment and because Hitler's attempts to reverse its terms brought him into conflict with Britain and France.</p><p><b>Paragraph:</b> The Treaty of Versailles led to World War Two because it created lasting German resentment and because Hitler's attempts to reverse its terms brought him into conflict with Britain and France. Firstly, Article 231, reparations of 132 billion gold marks and the loss of 13 per cent of German territory were seen as a Diktat, and the Nazis won votes by promising to cancel it. Secondly, once in power Hitler broke the treaty step by step: he rearmed from 1935, reoccupied the Rhineland in 1936 and united with Austria in 1938. Each success encouraged the next demand, until the invasion of Poland in September 1939 led Britain and France to declare war.</p>"}]
});
L.push({id:"2.14",t:"So what? analysis",
cards:[
 ["The most common weakness","Many answers describe what happened and stop. They give the evidence but do not say why it matters. Analysis is the sentence that links your evidence back to the question. Asking ‘so what?’ after each piece of evidence produces that sentence."],
 ["The table","For any event, fill in three boxes.","• <b>Cause:</b> what made it happen.","• <b>Effect:</b> what changed straight away.","• <b>So what?</b> why it matters and what it led to."],
 ["Phrases that begin analysis","This mattered because… This led to… As a result… This shows that… This weakened… This allowed Hitler to…","Use one of these after every piece of evidence in an explain, analyse or evaluate answer."]],
quiz:[
 ["What is analysis?","Saying why the evidence matters to the question","Listing dates","Copying the source","Describing a picture","It links evidence back to the question."],
 ["Which sentence is analysis?","This allowed Hitler to make laws without the Reichstag","The Enabling Act was passed on 23 March 1933","The vote was 444 to 94","The Reichstag met in an opera house","The others state facts. This one says what the fact led to."],
 ["When should you ask ‘so what?’","After each piece of evidence","Only at the end","Before reading the question","Never in a short answer","Every point needs a link back to the question."]],
practice:[
 {kind:"sowhat",q:"Fill in a ‘So what?’ table for German rearmament.",
  model:"<p><b>Cause:</b> Hitler wanted to reverse the Treaty of Versailles and prepare Germany for expansion.</p><p><b>Effect:</b> In 1935 Hitler announced conscription, and the army grew from the treaty limit of 100,000 men to 550,000.</p><p><b>So what?</b> Britain and France protested but did not act, which showed Hitler that the treaty would not be enforced. Rearmament gave him the forces to reoccupy the Rhineland in 1936 and to threaten his neighbours, and it reduced unemployment at home, which increased his popularity.</p>"},
 {kind:"sowhat",q:"Fill in a ‘So what?’ table for the Munich Agreement.",
  model:"<p><b>Cause:</b> Hitler demanded the Sudetenland, the German-speaking border region of Czechoslovakia, and Britain and France wanted to avoid war.</p><p><b>Effect:</b> On 30 September 1938 Britain, France, Italy and Germany agreed that Germany would take the Sudetenland. Czechoslovakia was not consulted.</p><p><b>So what?</b> Munich convinced Hitler that Britain and France would not fight. In March 1939 he took the rest of the Czech lands, which showed that appeasement had failed and led Britain and France to promise to defend Poland.</p>"}]
});
